exports[`renderRunbook for a server the user owns > matches the snapshots > public 1`] = `
"# Runbook: shop

Generated by OpsCrew 0.1.0 from \`opscrew/plan.yaml\`. Run \`opscrew verify\` to refresh it.

## At a glance

| | |
|---|---|
| App | https://example.com |
| Health check | \`/api/health\` |
| Server | your own server (amd64); OpsCrew prepared it over SSH and never creates, rebuilds or deletes it |
| IPv4 / IPv6 | kept private behind Cloudflare: \`opscrew status\` shows them |
| Deploys | every push to \`main\` runs \`.github/workflows/opscrew-deploy.yml\` |
| Image | \`ghcr.io/acme/shop\` (GitHub Container Registry) |
| Database | PostgreSQL 17 on the server, data in \`/srv/data/shop\` (see Components); backups are your responsibility |
| DNS | Cloudflare, proxied (the server's address is hidden behind Cloudflare) |
| Cost | no server price: you already pay for this server; OpsCrew adds no server cost |

## How a deploy works

1. You push to \`main\` (or run the "OpsCrew deploy" workflow by hand in GitHub → Actions).
2. GitHub Actions builds the Docker image from your Dockerfile and pushes it to GitHub Container Registry.
3. It copies \`opscrew/runtime/compose.yaml\` (with the services' settings) to \`/srv/apps/shop\` on the server and
   the app's site file to the shared edge in \`/srv/edge\`, writes the app's and the services' environment from the
   deploy secrets, starts the services, runs the release command if the app has one, switches to the new version,
   then restarts the workers and cron jobs with it. A worker that does not stay up sends the app back to the
   previous version.
4. It checks \`/api/health\`. If the app does not answer within three minutes, it restores the previous
   version automatically and the run fails.

## Roll back

- **Fastest:** in GitHub → Actions, open the last successful "OpsCrew deploy" run and choose **Re-run all jobs**.
- **With git:** revert the bad commit and push to \`main\`; the workflow deploys the reverted code.

## Components

The app runs as the Docker Compose project \`shop\` in \`/srv/apps/shop\` on the server. Each component is a
container with its own memory limit, and none publishes a port: only the edge (below) takes web traffic.

| Component | Runs | Memory limit | Data |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`app\` | the web process | 512 MB | |
| \`db\` | PostgreSQL 17 (PostgreSQL) | 512 MB | \`/srv/data/shop/db\` |

\`opscrew status\` shows whether each component runs, and \`docker compose logs <component>\` in the app's folder shows
its log. Change a component's count, memory or version, or turn one off, in \`opscrew/plan.yaml\`, then run
\`opscrew design\` (it checks that everything fits the server and keeps your changes) and \`opscrew provision\`.

## The shared edge

Web traffic reaches the app through OpsCrew's edge in \`/srv/edge\`, the Compose project \`opscrew-edge\`: Caddy
serves HTTPS and passes each request to kamal-proxy, which switches deploys without downtime. The app's site is
\`/srv/edge/sites/shop.caddy\` (from \`opscrew/runtime/edge/sites/shop.caddy\`). Each deploy copies the edge's files
from \`opscrew/runtime/edge/\` and replaces the edge only with a newer version, so an older app never downgrades it.
Every OpsCrew app on this server sits behind the same edge, each with its own folders in \`/srv/apps\` and
\`/srv/data\` and its own site file. \`opscrew destroy\` removes the edge only with the last app.

## Connect to the server

Your own login keeps working as before (with your key). To work as the deploy user:

\`\`\`bash
ssh -i ~/.opscrew/projects/shop-0123456789ab/deploy_key deploy@<server>
cd /srv/apps/shop
docker compose ps                 # what is running
docker compose logs -f app        # follow the app's logs
docker compose restart app        # restart the app
\`\`\`

\`<server>\` is the address you gave OpsCrew; it is not written in this repository. \`opscrew status\` shows it on the
machine that ran \`opscrew provision\`.
Cloudflare's proxy hides the address, and a published address would let anyone go around Cloudflare.

The deploy key is stored only on the machine where you ran \`opscrew provision\` (and as a GitHub Actions
secret). OpsCrew turned off password logins on the server.

## Backups are your responsibility

OpsCrew cannot turn on backups or snapshots on a server you own, and \`opscrew destroy\` takes no snapshot. Back up
the server the way your hosting provider or your own setup allows, and keep copies somewhere else.

Back up the database regularly, for example:

\`\`\`bash
# On the server: dump the database to a file
docker compose exec -T db sh -c 'pg_dump -U "$POSTGRES_USER" "$POSTGRES_DB"' > backup.sql
# Restore a dump
docker compose exec -T db sh -c 'psql -U "$POSTGRES_USER" "$POSTGRES_DB"' < backup.sql
\`\`\`

## Change settings and secrets

- **App environment variables:** run \`opscrew provision\` again; it updates the GitHub deploy secrets. Then re-run the
  deploy workflow so the server picks them up.
- **Domain or tunnel:** run \`opscrew design\` and \`opscrew provision\`. OpsCrew shows the plans (the Cloudflare changes and
  the host changes) and applies nothing without your approval.
- **Switching between direct access and the tunnel** runs in two steps: OpsCrew first removes the current way in
  (the DNS records, or the tunnel and its Access applications), then adds the new one, each with its own plan and
  approval. The app is not reachable on its domain between the two steps.
- **Your server itself** (size, operating system, other software) is yours to manage; OpsCrew changes only what its
  host plan lists, and records each change in \`/etc/opscrew/host.json\` on the server.
- **Infrastructure code:** \`opscrew/infra/\` is plain Terraform/OpenTofu that you own. You can run it yourself; state is
  kept in \`.opscrew/tfstate/\` on the machine that ran \`opscrew provision\`.

## Check for drift

Run \`opscrew drift\` on the machine that ran \`opscrew provision\`. It compares:

- **Cloudflare with the state and the code:** DNS records changed
  outside OpsCrew, or changes in \`opscrew/infra/\` that have not been applied yet.
- **The server with what OpsCrew set up:** SSH settings, firewall rules, the deploy user, runtime files and containers,
  against the record in \`/etc/opscrew/host.json\`.
- **The files with the plan:** files under \`opscrew/infra/\` and \`opscrew/runtime/\` that differ from what
  \`opscrew/plan.yaml\` would generate.

\`opscrew drift\` never changes anything. To fix drift, run \`opscrew provision\`, which shows the plans and applies
nothing without your approval.

## Security

- Run \`opscrew secure\` for a fresh security report (\`opscrew/SECURITY_REPORT.md\`) and \`opscrew verify\` to check DNS,
  HTTPS, the health endpoint and security headers.
- What OpsCrew set up on the server: Docker Engine from Docker's repository, a \`deploy\` user with key-only SSH,
  password logins off (root keeps key-only access only if you log in as root), automatic security updates and,
  unless another firewall already manages the server, a host firewall that allows only SSH, HTTP and HTTPS.
- Only Caddy publishes ports (80 and 443); every other container port stays on the server.
- Web traffic comes through Cloudflare's proxy; keep the server's address private so nobody goes around it.

## If something is wrong

1. Open the latest "OpsCrew deploy" run in GitHub → Actions and read the failed step.
2. Run \`opscrew verify\` to see which check fails (DNS, certificate, health endpoint …).
3. On the server, \`docker compose logs --tail 200 app\` usually shows why the app does not start.

## Tear it down

Run \`opscrew destroy\` on the machine that ran \`opscrew provision\`. It removes what OpsCrew set up for this app: the
Cloudflare DNS records and rules OpsCrew created, the app's containers and runtime
files on the server, and the GitHub deploy secrets. It **never** deletes, rebuilds or reinstalls your server.

- **App data:** the data in \`/srv/data/shop\` and the app's Docker volumes stay unless you add
  \`--delete-data\`, which needs the destroy approval and the typed app name (\`shop\`; \`--confirm shop\` in scripts).
  There is no snapshot: back up first if you want to keep it. Other apps on the server keep their data.
- **Undo OpsCrew's host changes:** \`opscrew destroy --undo-host\` also plans the reverse of what OpsCrew changed on the
  server (deploy user, SSH settings, firewall, automatic updates, Docker if OpsCrew installed it), from the record in
  \`/etc/opscrew/host.json\`. It is a separate plan that you approve on its own.
- **What remains:** your server, the container images in GitHub Container Registry (\`ghcr.io/acme/shop\`), and
  the files in this repository (\`opscrew/\` and \`.github/workflows/opscrew-deploy.yml\`).

To deploy again later, run \`opscrew provision\`.
"
`;

exports[`renderRunbook for a server the user owns > matches the snapshots > tunnel with Portainer 1`] = `
"# Runbook: shop

Generated by OpsCrew 0.1.0 from \`opscrew/plan.yaml\`. Run \`opscrew verify\` to refresh it.

## At a glance

| | |
|---|---|
| App | https://shop.example.com |
| Health check | \`/api/health\` |
| Server | your own server (arm64); OpsCrew prepared it over SSH and never creates, rebuilds or deletes it |
| IPv4 / IPv6 | none needed: the server is reached through a Cloudflare Tunnel |
| SSH for deploys | \`ssh-shop.example.com\`, through the tunnel and Cloudflare Access (only the deploy workflow's service token gets in) |
| Deploys | every push to \`main\` runs \`.github/workflows/opscrew-deploy.yml\` |
| Image | \`ghcr.io/acme/shop\` (GitHub Container Registry) |
| Database | PostgreSQL 17 on the server, data in \`/srv/data/shop\` (see Components); backups are your responsibility |
| DNS | Cloudflare, through a Cloudflare Tunnel (no DNS record points at the server) |
| Portainer | https://portainer.example.com (Cloudflare Access) and through an SSH tunnel |
| Cost | no server price: you already pay for this server; OpsCrew adds no server cost |

## How a deploy works

1. You push to \`main\` (or run the "OpsCrew deploy" workflow by hand in GitHub → Actions).
2. GitHub Actions builds the Docker image from your Dockerfile and pushes it to GitHub Container Registry.
   Your server is arm64, so the image is built on GitHub's arm64 runner.
3. It copies \`opscrew/runtime/compose.yaml\` (with the services' settings) to \`/srv/apps/shop\` on the server and
   the app's site file to the shared edge in \`/srv/edge\`, writes the app's and the services' environment from the
   deploy secrets, starts the services, runs the release command if the app has one, switches to the new version,
   then restarts the workers and cron jobs with it. A worker that does not stay up sends the app back to the
   previous version.
   Your server has no open port for this: the workflow installs a pinned \`cloudflared\` and connects to \`ssh-shop.example.com\`
   through the Cloudflare Tunnel with a Cloudflare Access service token kept in the deploy secrets.
4. It checks \`/api/health\`. If the app does not answer within three minutes, it restores the previous
   version automatically and the run fails.

## Roll back

- **Fastest:** in GitHub → Actions, open the last successful "OpsCrew deploy" run and choose **Re-run all jobs**.
- **With git:** revert the bad commit and push to \`main\`; the workflow deploys the reverted code.

## Components

The app runs as the Docker Compose project \`shop\` in \`/srv/apps/shop\` on the server. Each component is a
container with its own memory limit, and none publishes a port: only the edge (below) takes web traffic.

| Component | Runs | Memory limit | Data |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`app\` | the web process | 512 MB | |
| \`db\` | PostgreSQL 17 (PostgreSQL) | 512 MB | \`/srv/data/shop/db\` |

\`opscrew status\` shows whether each component runs, and \`docker compose logs <component>\` in the app's folder shows
its log. Change a component's count, memory or version, or turn one off, in \`opscrew/plan.yaml\`, then run
\`opscrew design\` (it checks that everything fits the server and keeps your changes) and \`opscrew provision\`.

## The shared edge

Web traffic reaches the app through OpsCrew's edge in \`/srv/edge\`, the Compose project \`opscrew-edge\`: Caddy
serves HTTPS and passes each request to kamal-proxy, which switches deploys without downtime. The app's site is
\`/srv/edge/sites/shop.caddy\` (from \`opscrew/runtime/edge/sites/shop.caddy\`). Each deploy copies the edge's files
from \`opscrew/runtime/edge/\` and replaces the edge only with a newer version, so an older app never downgrades it.
Every OpsCrew app on this server sits behind the same edge, each with its own folders in \`/srv/apps\` and
\`/srv/data\` and its own site file. \`opscrew destroy\` removes the edge only with the last app.

## Connect to the server

Your own login keeps working as before (with your key). To work as the deploy user:

\`\`\`bash
ssh -i ~/.opscrew/projects/shop-0123456789ab/deploy_key deploy@<server>
cd /srv/apps/shop
docker compose ps                 # what is running
docker compose logs -f app        # follow the app's logs
docker compose restart app        # restart the app
\`\`\`

\`<server>\` is the address you gave OpsCrew; it is not written in this repository. \`opscrew status\` shows it on the
machine that ran \`opscrew provision\`.
The tunnel's SSH hostname (\`ssh-shop.example.com\`) only lets the deploy workflow in; connect over your own network
or VPN as you did when you ran \`opscrew provision\`.

The deploy key is stored only on the machine where you ran \`opscrew provision\` (and as a GitHub Actions
secret). OpsCrew turned off password logins on the server.

## Backups are your responsibility

OpsCrew cannot turn on backups or snapshots on a server you own, and \`opscrew destroy\` takes no snapshot. Back up
the server the way your hosting provider or your own setup allows, and keep copies somewhere else.

Back up the database regularly, for example:

\`\`\`bash
# On the server: dump the database to a file
docker compose exec -T db sh -c 'pg_dump -U "$POSTGRES_USER" "$POSTGRES_DB"' > backup.sql
# Restore a dump
docker compose exec -T db sh -c 'psql -U "$POSTGRES_USER" "$POSTGRES_DB"' < backup.sql
\`\`\`

## Portainer

Portainer CE 2.33.1 shows your server's containers. It listens only on the server itself
(127.0.0.1:9443), because it controls Docker, which is root on the server. To open it, start an SSH tunnel
(\`opscrew portainer status\` prints this command for you):

\`\`\`bash
ssh -i ~/.opscrew/projects/shop-0123456789ab/deploy_key -N -L 9443:127.0.0.1:9443 deploy@<server>
\`\`\`

Then open https://localhost:9443 (Portainer uses a self-signed certificate) and log in as \`admin\`.
The password is in \`~/.opscrew/projects/shop-0123456789ab/portainer_password\`
on the machine that ran \`opscrew provision\`; it is never written to this repository.

It is also served on https://portainer.example.com, behind Cloudflare Access: only the email addresses listed in
\`opscrew/plan.yaml\` get in, with a one-time PIN sent to that address, and Portainer's own login still applies.

\`opscrew portainer disable\` removes it (deleting its data only after you type the app name).

## Change settings and secrets

- **App environment variables:** run \`opscrew provision\` again; it updates the GitHub deploy secrets. Then re-run the
  deploy workflow so the server picks them up.
- **Domain or tunnel:** run \`opscrew design\` and \`opscrew provision\`. OpsCrew shows the plans (the Cloudflare changes and
  the host changes) and applies nothing without your approval.
- **Switching between direct access and the tunnel** runs in two steps: OpsCrew first removes the current way in
  (the DNS records, or the tunnel and its Access applications), then adds the new one, each with its own plan and
  approval. The app is not reachable on its domain between the two steps.
- **Your server itself** (size, operating system, other software) is yours to manage; OpsCrew changes only what its
  host plan lists, and records each change in \`/etc/opscrew/host.json\` on the server.
- **Infrastructure code:** \`opscrew/infra/\` is plain Terraform/OpenTofu that you own. You can run it yourself; state is
  kept in \`.opscrew/tfstate/\` on the machine that ran \`opscrew provision\`.

## Check for drift

Run \`opscrew drift\` on the machine that ran \`opscrew provision\`. It compares:

- **Cloudflare with the state and the code:** DNS records, the tunnel and Cloudflare Access changed
  outside OpsCrew, or changes in \`opscrew/infra/\` that have not been applied yet.
- **The server with what OpsCrew set up:** SSH settings, firewall rules, the deploy user, runtime files and containers,
  against the record in \`/etc/opscrew/host.json\`.
- **The files with the plan:** files under \`opscrew/infra/\` and \`opscrew/runtime/\` that differ from what
  \`opscrew/plan.yaml\` would generate.

\`opscrew drift\` never changes anything. To fix drift, run \`opscrew provision\`, which shows the plans and applies
nothing without your approval.

## Security

- Run \`opscrew secure\` for a fresh security report (\`opscrew/SECURITY_REPORT.md\`) and \`opscrew verify\` to check DNS,
  HTTPS, the health endpoint and security headers.
- What OpsCrew set up on the server: Docker Engine from Docker's repository, a \`deploy\` user with key-only SSH,
  password logins off (root keeps key-only access only if you log in as root), automatic security updates and,
  unless another firewall already manages the server, a host firewall that allows only SSH.
- No inbound web port is open: Cloudflare's edge reaches the app only through the tunnel, which the server dials out to.

## If something is wrong

1. Open the latest "OpsCrew deploy" run in GitHub → Actions and read the failed step.
2. Run \`opscrew verify\` to see which check fails (DNS, certificate, health endpoint …).
3. On the server, \`docker compose logs --tail 200 app\` usually shows why the app does not start.

## Tear it down

Run \`opscrew destroy\` on the machine that ran \`opscrew provision\`. It removes what OpsCrew set up for this app: the
Cloudflare tunnel, Access applications and DNS records OpsCrew created, the app's containers and runtime
files on the server, and the GitHub deploy secrets. It **never** deletes, rebuilds or reinstalls your server.

- **App data:** the data in \`/srv/data/shop\` and the app's Docker volumes stay unless you add
  \`--delete-data\`, which needs the destroy approval and the typed app name (\`shop\`; \`--confirm shop\` in scripts).
  There is no snapshot: back up first if you want to keep it. Other apps on the server keep their data.
- **Undo OpsCrew's host changes:** \`opscrew destroy --undo-host\` also plans the reverse of what OpsCrew changed on the
  server (deploy user, SSH settings, firewall, automatic updates, Docker if OpsCrew installed it), from the record in
  \`/etc/opscrew/host.json\`. It is a separate plan that you approve on its own.
- **What remains:** your server, the container images in GitHub Container Registry (\`ghcr.io/acme/shop\`), and
  the files in this repository (\`opscrew/\` and \`.github/workflows/opscrew-deploy.yml\`).

To deploy again later, run \`opscrew provision\`.
"
`;
